description:
Thanks a lot for all the work you've put into plotnine. I've been using it for a while now and it's been awesome for visualizing my data in Python.

I wanted to suggest adding a symlog scale option in plotnine. Symlog scaling is a pretty neat way to display data that has a lot of different orders of magnitude, including zero and negative numbers. Matplotlib already has this feature and it's been super useful in my projects. You can check out matplotlib's doc [here](https://matplotlib.org/3.1.0/gallery/scales/symlog_demo.html) for reference.

To help things along, here's some Python code that implements the symlog transformation:

```python
from plotnine.scales.scale import scale_y_continuous
from mizani.transforms import trans

class SymLogTransform(trans):
    def transform(self, x):
        return np.sign(x) * np.log1p(np.abs(x))

    def inverse(self, x):
        return np.sign(x) * (np.exp(np.abs(x)) - 1)

class scale_y_symlog(scale_y_continuous):
    def __init__(self, **kwargs):
        super().__init__(trans=SymLogTransform(), **kwargs)
```

I've been using this code as a workaround, but it'd be great if symlog was built right into plotnine.

Let me know if you're planning to add this or if there's another way to achieve the same result. And if there's anything I can do to help get this feature added, please do let me know!
